### What is an anonymous class?

An anonymous class is a local class without a name that you declare and instantiate in one expression. You write `new SuperType(args) { body }`, and the compiler creates a hidden subclass, instantiates it, and hands you the reference. The class exists only at that point in the code and cannot be reused elsewhere by name.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class TradeService {
    public void sortTradesByAmount(List<Trade> trades) {
        // Anonymous class implementing Comparator
        trades.sort(new Comparator<Trade>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
                return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
            }
        });
    }
}
```

The object is created immediately. The class has no name you can refer to anywhere else in the code.

### Why do we need anonymous classes?

Anonymous classes exist for situations where you need a one-off implementation that is too small to justify a named class file. Before Java 8 lambdas, every callback, listener, and comparator was written as an anonymous class. Even after lambdas arrived, anonymous classes remain necessary when the target interface has more than one abstract method, or when you need to extend a concrete class rather than implement a functional interface.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    interface TransferListener {
        void onStart(String fromAccount, String toAccount);
        void onComplete(BigDecimal amount);
    }

    public void executeTransfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Two abstract methods: lambda cannot implement this
        TransferListener listener = new TransferListener() {
            @Override
            public void onStart(String fromAccount, String toAccount) {
                System.out.println("Transfer started: " + fromAccount + " -> " + toAccount);
            }

            @Override
            public void onComplete(BigDecimal transferredAmount) {
                System.out.println("Transfer complete: " + transferredAmount + " INR");
            }
        };
        listener.onStart(from, to);
        // ... perform transfer logic ...
        listener.onComplete(amount);
    }
}
```

### How do you create an anonymous class?

The syntax is `new SuperType(constructorArgs) { body }`. When `SuperType` is a class, the constructor arguments go to the superclass constructor. When `SuperType` is an interface, the argument list must be empty because interfaces have no constructors.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountDemo {

    static abstract class Account {
        String id;
        BigDecimal balance;

        Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
            this.id = id;
            this.balance = balance;
        }

        abstract BigDecimal monthlyFee();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Anonymous class extending a class with constructor args
        Account priyaAccount = new Account("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("500000")) {
            @Override
            BigDecimal monthlyFee() {
                return new BigDecimal("150.00");
            }
        };

        System.out.println("Account: " + priyaAccount.id);
        System.out.println("Monthly fee: " + priyaAccount.monthlyFee());
    }
}
```

```text
Account: SAV-1001
Monthly fee: 150.00
```

### What are the restrictions of anonymous classes?

Anonymous classes have four key restrictions.

- **No explicit constructor.** The class has no name, so you cannot write a constructor. Use an instance initializer block for setup logic.
- **Single base type.** You can extend one class or implement one interface, never both, and never multiple interfaces.
- **No static members.** You cannot declare static methods or mutable static fields. Only compile-time constant `static final` fields are allowed.
- **Effectively-final capture.** Local variables referenced inside the anonymous body must never be reassigned after initialization.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RestrictionDemo {

    interface FeeCalculator {
        BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal);
    }

    public void demo() {
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.02"); // effectively final

        FeeCalculator calc = new FeeCalculator() {
            // Instance initializer instead of constructor
            {
                System.out.println("FeeCalculator initialized");
            }

            // Allowed: compile-time constant
            static final String TYPE = "PROCESSING_FEE";

            // Not allowed: static method
            // static void helper() { } // compile error

            @Override
            public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal) {
                return principal.multiply(rate); // captures effectively-final rate
            }
        };

        System.out.println(calc.calculate(new BigDecimal("100000")));
    }
}
```

```text
FeeCalculator initialized
2000.00
```

### How does an anonymous class compare to a lambda?

The core difference is that a lambda can only implement a functional interface (one abstract method), whereas an anonymous class can implement multi-method interfaces or extend a concrete class.

| Feature | Anonymous Class | Lambda |
|---|---|---|
| Target | Any class or interface | Functional interface only |
| `this` keyword | Refers to anonymous object | Refers to enclosing instance |
| Constructor | No (use initializer block) | No |
| State (fields) | Can declare instance fields | No fields |
| Static members | Only constant `static final` | No |
| Bytecode | Generates `Outer$1.class` | Uses `invokedynamic` |
| Memory | Heavier (new class per site) | Lighter (shared at runtime) |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.function.Function;

public class LambdaVsAnonymous {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("250000");

        // Lambda: functional interface with one method
        Function<BigDecimal, BigDecimal> lambdaFee = amount ->
                amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.01"));

        // Anonymous class: same result but more verbose
        Function<BigDecimal, BigDecimal> anonFee = new Function<BigDecimal, BigDecimal>() {
            @Override
            public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal amount) {
                return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.01"));
            }
        };

        System.out.println("Lambda fee:    " + lambdaFee.apply(balance));
        System.out.println("Anonymous fee: " + anonFee.apply(balance));
    }
}
```

```text
Lambda fee:    2500.00
Anonymous fee: 2500.00
```

### What are common mistakes with anonymous classes?

The most frequent mistake is trying to reassign a captured local variable, which breaks the effectively-final rule.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CommonMistakes {

    interface ResultCallback {
        void onResult(BigDecimal amount);
    }

    // WRONG: reassigning captured variable
    public void wrongApproach() {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        ResultCallback cb = new ResultCallback() {
            @Override
            public void onResult(BigDecimal amount) {
                // total = total.add(amount); // COMPILE ERROR
            }
        };
        total = new BigDecimal("100"); // reassignment makes it non-effectively-final
    }

    // CORRECT: use a mutable holder
    public void correctApproach() {
        BigDecimal[] total = { BigDecimal.ZERO }; // array reference is effectively final
        ResultCallback cb = new ResultCallback() {
            @Override
            public void onResult(BigDecimal amount) {
                total[0] = total[0].add(amount); // mutate array contents
            }
        };
        cb.onResult(new BigDecimal("50000"));
        System.out.println("Total: " + total[0]);
    }
}
```

### When should you use an anonymous class?

Use an anonymous class when you need a one-off implementation of a multi-method interface or must extend a concrete class inline. If the target is a functional interface with a single abstract method, prefer a lambda because it is shorter, clearer, and lighter at runtime.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class UsageGuideline {

    // Multi-method interface: anonymous class is the right tool
    interface AuditCallback {
        void beforeAudit(String accountId);
        void afterAudit(String accountId, BigDecimal auditedBalance);
    }

    public void auditAccount(String accountId) {
        AuditCallback callback = new AuditCallback() {
            @Override
            public void beforeAudit(String id) {
                System.out.println("Starting audit for " + id);
            }

            @Override
            public void afterAudit(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
                System.out.println("Audit complete for " + id + ": " + balance + " INR");
            }
        };
        callback.beforeAudit(accountId);
        callback.afterAudit(accountId, new BigDecimal("1500000"));
    }
}
```

### What about thread safety and concurrency?

An anonymous class that captures effectively-final variables holds a stable snapshot, which is inherently safe to pass to another thread. If you use a mutable holder like `AtomicReference<BigDecimal>` for shared state, you must handle thread safety yourself.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;

public class ThreadSafeAnonymous {

    public void processBatchTransfers() {
        AtomicReference<BigDecimal> totalTransferred =
                new AtomicReference<>(BigDecimal.ZERO);

        Runnable task = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("25000");
                totalTransferred.updateAndGet(t -> t.add(amount));
                System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                        + " transferred " + amount + " INR");
            }
        };

        Thread t1 = new Thread(task, "Worker-1");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task, "Worker-2");
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}
```

### What about serialization?

Anonymous classes can implement `Serializable`, but serialization captures the enclosing instance and all captured variables. If the enclosing class is not serializable, you get a `NotSerializableException`. In banking systems, prefer named classes for anything that crosses a serialization boundary.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.io.Serializable;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SerializationWarning {

    // This anonymous class captures the enclosing SerializationWarning instance
    Serializable feeRule = new Serializable() {
        private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
        BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.015");

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return "FeeRule{rate=" + rate + "}";
        }
    };
    // If SerializationWarning is not Serializable, writing feeRule
    // to an ObjectOutputStream throws NotSerializableException
}
```
